# CompTIA A+ — Operating Systems

Which Windows command scans and repairs protected system files?
---
sfc /scannow
= sfc
---
System File Checker. If it fails, DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth repairs the image it draws from.

Which Windows command checks a disk for file system errors?
---
chkdsk
---
chkdsk /f fixes errors, /r also locates bad sectors.

Which Windows command forces an immediate Group Policy refresh?
---
gpupdate
= gpupdate /force

Which Windows utility manages startup programs, services, and running processes?
---
Task Manager
= task manager
---
Ctrl+Shift+Esc. Startup items moved here from msconfig in Windows 8.

Which Windows console holds Device Manager, Disk Management, and Event Viewer in one window?
---
Computer Management
= compmgmt.msc

Which file system does Windows use by default on internal drives?
---
NTFS
---
Supports permissions, encryption, compression, and journaling. FAT32 caps files at 4 GB.

Which file system works across Windows, macOS, and Linux for large files on a flash drive?
---
exFAT
---
FAT32 is equally portable but cannot hold a file over 4 GB.

Which Windows edition is the minimum for joining a domain?
---
Pro
= Windows Pro
= professional
---
Home cannot join a domain and lacks BitLocker and the Group Policy editor.

Which Linux command lists the files in a directory?
---
ls

Which Linux command changes file permissions?
---
chmod
---
chown changes the owner instead.

Which Linux command shows how much free disk space each filesystem has?
---
df
= df -h

Which Linux command searches text for lines matching a pattern?
---
grep

Which Linux command elevates a single command to root privileges?
---
sudo

Which macOS feature is its built-in backup tool?
---
Time Machine
= time machine
---
Hourly incremental backups to an external or network drive.

Which macOS file format is used to distribute most application installers?
---
.dmg
= dmg
= disk image
---
Mount the disk image, drag the .app to Applications, eject.

Windows full-drive encryption is called {{BitLocker}}.
---
Pro editions and up. macOS calls its equivalent FileVault.
